1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,217
Most AI agents answer every question with the same expensive model.

2
00:00:04,217 --> 00:00:10,255
Here's a router that picks the right one before the conversation starts, and proves what each answer cost.

3
00:00:12,555 --> 00:00:14,193
First, a quick health check.

4
00:00:14,193 --> 00:00:17,761
The API key works, and all three Claude models are available.

5
00:00:18,861 --> 00:00:19,928
A simple question:

6
00:00:19,928 --> 00:00:21,469
what is compound interest?

7
00:00:21,469 --> 00:00:24,374
The router sends it to Haiku, the cheapest model.

8
00:00:24,374 --> 00:00:29,058
Hermes opens with the question already asked, and answers in about two seconds.

9
00:00:35,884 --> 00:00:36,853
Now a harder one:

10
00:00:36,853 --> 00:00:40,789
a team that keeps missing deadlines, and a thirty-day plan to fix it.

11
00:00:40,789 --> 00:00:44,667
This needs judgement, so the router picks Opus, the strongest model.

12
00:00:44,667 --> 00:00:46,892
It takes longer, and gives a real plan.

13
00:01:03,404 --> 00:01:04,483
Notice the footer:

14
00:01:04,483 --> 00:01:07,839
Opus itself says a cheaper model would have been enough.

15
00:01:07,839 --> 00:01:09,697
The agent is honest about cost.

16
00:01:13,884 --> 00:01:17,843
Here's what each answer actually cost, from the records Hermes keeps.

17
00:01:17,843 --> 00:01:23,121
Both figures are recomputed independently from Anthropic's published prices, and they match.

18
00:01:24,221 --> 00:01:27,016
The simple answer cost about a quarter of a cent.

19
00:01:27,016 --> 00:01:29,183
The complex one, about fourteen cents.

20
00:01:29,183 --> 00:01:32,434
Fifty times more, and only when the question deserves it.

21
00:01:33,534 --> 00:01:37,460
This budget router for Hermes is free and open source on GitHub.
